cartridge "SNAP SNAKE/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1988"

' SNAP SNAKE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Snap Snake" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! June 1988 (Commodore 64: BASIC with a machine-language interrupt
' that walks the rats and plays the music). Made for Amy Studio by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' listing and its machine code, orig/), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a snake whose head glides cell to cell, its body drawn in
' joined pieces, rats as animated sprites, brick walls that change colour
' every level, sound effects.
'
' The game: the snap snake lives in a den in the middle of a field of brick
' blocks. Push the stick and it grows out of its hole, a cell at a time,
' along the passages; hold a button and it snaps back, a cell at a time.
' Eat the white eggs hidden in the blocks (50 points each); frogs are worth
' 100 x the level, the jewel is a mystery (200 to 1000). Rats run up, down
' and across the open lanes between the blocks and never stop; a rat that
' touches any part of the snake bites it, and the snake is lost (three
' snakes). When every white egg is eaten the snake goes home, more eggs are
' laid, the walls change colour and the rats run faster.
'
' From the original (orig/): the snake grows only into an empty cell (or an
' egg, a frog, the jewel), only up, down, left or right, at most 200 cells,
' and never moves by itself; the button pulls it back a cell at a time; a
' rat touching the snake ends it; eggs 50, frogs 100 x level, the jewel
' 200 x (1 to 5) at random; level n lays 4n + 1 eggs (n from 0) on a list
' of hiding places, then 4 frogs (on an empty place or a frog), then one
' try for the jewel (an empty place only); frogs and jewels left over stay;
' the level goes up to 9; four rats of one set run on even levels and the
' other four on odd levels (all eight keep running, as in the interrupt),
' each in a straight line round and round, at a pixel every 9 - level
' sixtieths of a second; three snakes; a high score.
'
' Liberties: the maze is redrawn for the 32-column screen (nine blocks, two
' lanes each way, a corridor all round, the den in the middle, 46 hiding
' places for eggs); the C64's title card says 10 points an egg and 50 x
' level a frog, the program gives 50 and 100 x (level + 1): the program's
' values are kept and shown; after a bite the new snake starts in the den
' (the listing went on from where the head had been, with an invisible
' head); on the stick's diagonals the snake takes the turn (the C64 did not
' move); the head always faces where it is going (the listing drew it facing
' up after every snap back); the collision is a box test on the lane cells
' (the C64 used the sprite-to-background collision register); no music
' during play (the C64 played a loop); nothing flashes.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick grows the snake, either button
' pulls it back. On the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given (bug
' 25). Own xorshift random numbers (bug 36).

